with an example of how Google
Earth Engine and Google

00:38:27.550 --> 00:38:30.340
Cloud enable customers
to assess risks

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:32.450
arising from climate change.

00:38:32.450 --> 00:38:34.480
But instead of me
telling you about it,

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:38.310
we've invited Joel
Conkling to show you.

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:39.780
JOEL CONKLING: Thanks, Aparna.

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:41.490
The world is
constantly changing,

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:44.460
and that creates
opportunities and risks.

00:38:44.460 --> 00:38:46.890
Helping uncover critical
insights about the changing

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:50.160
world is why we're integrating
Earth Engine into Google Cloud,

00:38:50.160 --> 00:38:53.460
and that integration is
now in Private Preview.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:57.240
Today, I'll demo a workflow
that combines Vertex AI, Earth

00:38:57.240 --> 00:39:01.080
Engine, BigQuery, and
Google Maps platform

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:03.480
to show how Google Cloud makes
it incredibly easy for you

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:07.270
to innovate, and deliver
insights, and do it quickly.

00:39:07.270 --> 00:39:08.820
So here's a scenario.

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:10.260
You work at an
insurance company,

00:39:10.260 --> 00:39:12.218
and you need to analyze
your company's exposure

00:39:12.218 --> 00:39:13.145
to flood risk.

00:39:13.145 --> 00:39:15.270
You think new buildings
may be a strong contributor

00:39:15.270 --> 00:39:18.810
to that risk, and you want
to test your hypothesis.

00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:22.230
To do that, we first need to
understand where the built

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:24.180
environment is expanding.

00:39:24.180 --> 00:39:26.610
In other words, we just need
to categorize the surface

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:28.650
of the entire planet.

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:30.690
That could be
hard, but Vertex AI

00:39:30.690 --> 00:39:34.080
offers the tooling to develop
a best in class ML model.

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:38.240
And Earth Engine provides
constantly updated data.

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:39.530
Let's fast forward a bit.

00:39:39.530 --> 00:39:42.500
We finished training our
model, and now, Earth Engine

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:44.780
is sending satellite
imagery to be categorized.

00:39:44.780 --> 00:39:46.490
So your understanding
of the world

00:39:46.490 --> 00:39:49.358
can update in near real time.

00:39:49.358 --> 00:39:50.650
Here's the Earth Engine script.

00:39:50.650 --> 00:39:52.630
I'm showing the
results of that model.

00:39:52.630 --> 00:39:55.450
This area in red is
where the model estimates

00:39:55.450 --> 00:39:56.860
the locations of buildings.

00:39:56.860 --> 00:39:59.860
That's your current
built environment.

00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:04.550
To find the change over time, we
need a few more lines of code.

00:40:04.550 --> 00:40:09.170
These lines of code give us
a built environment in 2016,

00:40:09.170 --> 00:40:11.540
and here, we calculate
the difference

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:14.120
between 2016 and today.

00:40:14.120 --> 00:40:18.080
When there's a change, it
shows up in purple on the map.

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:21.240
This is where there
are new buildings.

00:40:21.240 --> 00:40:24.540
So next, we're going to export,
sample, and export the data.

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:26.415
So we can do additional
analysis in BigQuery.

00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:31.190
Over in the BigQuery
console, this script

00:40:31.190 --> 00:40:35.000
clusters those data points
here and then outputs

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:37.820
polygons that show the areas
with the biggest changes

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:40.640
in the built environment.

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:42.350
So at this point, you
have a few options.

00:40:42.350 --> 00:40:44.560
You could combine this
data with flood locations

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:48.130
you identify around the world,
also, with Earth Engine.

00:40:48.130 --> 00:40:50.230
Maybe you want to enhance
your model with weather

00:40:50.230 --> 00:40:52.120
data and physical terrain data.

00:40:52.120 --> 00:40:54.520
That's available on
Earth Engine too.

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.980
You could also include data
on your company's insurance

00:40:56.980 --> 00:41:01.770
portfolio to gain additional
insight into critical risks.

00:41:01.770 --> 00:41:05.800
We'll wrap up this demo
by visualizing our results

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:08.530
in a new feature available
on Google Maps platform,

00:41:08.530 --> 00:41:10.750
the Open Source Data
Viz Library Deck

00:41:10.750 --> 00:41:14.380
GL with a BigQuery
connector provided by CARTO.

00:41:14.380 --> 00:41:17.410
So we now have a clear picture
of where the built environment

00:41:17.410 --> 00:41:20.680
is changing, and where to
focus next for our work

00:41:20.680 --> 00:41:22.420
on flood risks.

00:41:22.420 --> 00:41:25.660
In summary, no
wrangling data, no need

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:28.810
to manage infrastructure,
just actionable insights

00:41:28.810 --> 00:41:30.538
incredibly quickly.

00:41:30.538 --> 00:41:32.830
We can't wait to see what
you'll do with Earth Engine's

00:41:32.830 --> 00:41:34.810
new integration
with Google Cloud,

00:41:34.810 --> 00:41:39.002
